WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.494
File systems are not static text stores.

2
00:00:02.574 --> 00:00:06.269
Symbolic links, hard links, replacement,
rename behavior, mount points,

3
00:00:06.349 --> 00:00:09.165
and special files can complicate
path-based decisions.

4
00:00:09.245 --> 00:00:13.116
A path that appears to refer to an
ordinary file at one moment may not refer to the

5
00:00:13.196 --> 00:00:16.421
same thing later, especially in shared or
writable directories.

6
00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:19.317
A check performed before opening a file
can become stale.

7
00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:22.036
The program may validate a name, inspect a
candidate,

8
00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:24.462
or confirm that a location appears
acceptable,

9
00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:28.648
but another process or thread may change
the file system before the later operation

10
00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:29.138
occurs.

11
00:00:29.218 --> 00:00:33.148
That gap between check and use is where
fragile path workflows often break down.

12
00:00:33.228 --> 00:00:37.275
A defensive design avoids assuming that a
path checked earlier still refers to the

13
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:38.293
same object later.

14
00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:42.538
For sensitive operations, the program may
need platform-supported open patterns,

15
00:00:42.618 --> 00:00:45.316
directory-relative operations, restrictive
flags,

16
00:00:45.396 --> 00:00:48.856
or file identity checks that reduce
reliance on text-only decisions.

17
00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:52.925
The right mechanism depends on the
platform and the sensitivity of the workflow.

18
00:00:53.005 --> 00:00:56.524
Shared directories require extra care
because other users, services,

19
00:00:56.604 --> 00:00:58.305
or processes may influence names.

20
00:00:58.385 --> 00:01:00.848
Temporary work areas, upload staging
locations,

21
00:01:00.928 --> 00:01:05.151
and extraction directories should be
designed so the application controls creation

22
00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:06.932
and ownership as much as possible.

23
00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:09.123
If other actors can write in the same
place,

24
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:12.077
the program needs fewer assumptions and
stronger checks.

25
00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:15.911
Race-aware design is not about making the
code complicated for its own sake.

26
00:01:15.991 --> 00:01:20.038
It is about reducing the number of claims
the program makes between validation and

27
00:01:20.118 --> 00:01:20.353
use.

28
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:22.603
The closer the decision is to the
operation,

29
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:25.674
and the more the platform can enforce in
one controlled step,

30
00:01:25.754 --> 00:01:28.100
the less the program depends on stale path
text.
